---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Notes — CopperTrace">
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <div>
      <div class="kicker">Bench log</div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <p class="lead">Short measurements, experiments and useful technical findings that sit between a quick bench observation and a full project article.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-aside"><strong>Working notebook</strong>Notes are intentionally concise. Confirmed observations are separated from ideas that still need testing.</aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><div class="kicker">September 2026</div><h2>Latest bench notes</h2></div>
        <p>Small findings worth keeping, with links back to the larger projects where relevant.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <a class="card" href="/projects/toolkitrc-p200/">
          <div class="meta">21 Sep 2026 · P200</div>
          <h3>USB exposes a firmware-update volume</h3>
          <p>The P200 V1 enumerates as an STM32 USB Mass Storage device and mounts a tiny FAT12 volume labelled Toolkit. No obvious CDC serial or HID control interface was exposed.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">USB</span><span class="tag">Firmware</span><span class="tag">Linux</span></div>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="/projects/toolkitrc-p200/">
          <div class="meta">21 Sep 2026 · P200</div>
          <h3>V1 firmware identified</h3>
          <p>The correct package is ToolkitRC P200 V1.05-STN. Its app.upg image contains a Cortex-M vector table and useful plaintext strings related to output and USB charging.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">ARM</span><span class="tag">GD32</span><span class="tag">Reverse engineering</span></div>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="/projects/toolkitrc-p200/">
          <div class="meta">Next bench session · P200</div>
          <h3>Logic-analyser capture plan</h3>
          <p>Measure labelled test-point voltages first, then capture suspected control-bus traffic during boot, output switching, voltage changes and current-limit changes.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">sigrok</span><span class="tag">I²C</span><span class="tag">Test plan</span></div>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="/projects/sky-serpent/">
          <div class="meta">Sky Serpent · Active R&amp;D</div>
          <h3>Ethernet-first sensing</h3>
          <p>Sensor nodes are being developed around wired Ethernet so the measurement network does not unnecessarily depend on another wireless link in the RF environment under study.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Ethernet</span><span class="tag">Embedded</span><span class="tag">RF</span></div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><div class="kicker">How notes work</div><h2>Evidence before conclusions</h2></div>
        <p>A note may record a measurement, a failed approach or the next experiment. Once a result becomes substantial and repeatable, it can graduate into a full project article.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
